Forward Proxy vs Reverse Proxy – Explained with a Restaurant Example
Proxies are everywhere in tech, but the concept is easier to grasp if we map it to daily life.

🔹 Forward Proxy (for the Client) 
 Imagine you want food from a hotel 🍕, but you don’t want the hotel to know it’s you ordering.
So, you ask your friend to place the order for you.
The hotel sees the friend (forward proxy), not you.
Your friend collects the food and gives it back to you.

✅ Real-life usage:
Bypassing restrictions (friend can go where you can’t).
Anonymity (hotel never knows it was you).
Caching (friend remembers the order and brings it faster next time).

🔹 Reverse Proxy (for the Server)
 Now flip the scenario: you go to a big hotel 🍽️ with multiple chefs in the kitchen.
Instead of talking to each chef, you always talk to the waiter.
The waiter (reverse proxy) decides which chef will cook your order.
You only deal with the waiter, never directly with the chefs.

✅ Real-life usage:
Load balancing (waiter divides orders among chefs).
Security (you don’t know who the chefs are, they’re hidden).
SSL termination (waiter handles communication, chefs just cook).

📌 Key Difference 
Forward Proxy → Works on behalf of the client. It sends client requests to the internet and returns the response back.
Reverse Proxy → Works on behalf of the server. It accepts client requests, forwards them to the right server, and sends back the response.
